<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutesa I of Buganda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutesa I was kabaka of Buganda from 1856 to 1884. He strengthened royal power over the chiefs and opened his court to Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders, adopting some Islamic practices. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> publicised his interest in 1875, he received Protestant and Catholic missionaries, whose rivalry with Muslim converts at court led to civil war under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutesa-i-of-buganda.jpg" alt="Mutesa I of Buganda" width="150" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mutesa_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
